vg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The National Assembly of the Republic of Bulgaria (\u041d\u0430\u0440\u043e\u0434\u043d\u043e \u0441\u044a\u0431\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u043d\u0430 \u0420\u0435\u043f\u0443\u0431\u043b\u0438\u043a\u0430 \u0411\u044a\u043b\u0433\u0430\u0440\u0438\u044f, <i>Narodno Sabranie na Republika Bulgaria<\/i>) houses the Bulgarian unicameral parliament. My favorite part about the building is the slogan: Unity is what creates strength (\u0421\u044a\u0435\u0434\u0438\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435\u0442\u043e \u043f\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0438 \u0441\u0438\u043b\u0430\u0442\u0430, <i>Saedinenieto pravi silata<\/i>). The slogan managed to stay relevant through several different political regimes. It reflects on Bulgaria's tendency, as a small country, to team up with other countries without losing its independence.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2225.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral was completed in 1912 as a thank you to the Russians for their help in various wars. It is considered the tallest church on the Balkan Peninsula.\" data-attachment-id=\"1543\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral was completed in 1912 as a thank you to the Russians for their help in various wars. It is considered the tallest church on the Balkan Peninsula.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2225\/1710046114.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral was completed in 1912 as a thank you to the Russians for their help in various wars. It is considered the tallest church on the Balkan Peninsula.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2233.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The statue of the best known Bulgarian writer Ivan Vazov, often referred to as &quot;the Patriarch of Bulgarian literature&quot;. The reddish building in the background is Temple Sveta Sofia (\u0425\u0440\u0430\u043c \u201e\u0421\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0430 \u0421\u043e\u0444\u0438\u044f\u201c). It was constructed in the 6th century on top of existing places of worship, which dated all the way back to the Roman city of Serdika, during the second century. It is believed that during the 14th century, it gave its name to the capital. Finally, the church peeking through the trees is St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral.\" data-attachment-id=\"1523\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The statue of the best known Bulgarian writer Ivan Vazov, often referred to as &quot;the Patriarch of Bulgarian literature&quot;. The reddish building in the background is Temple Sveta Sofia (\u0425\u0440\u0430\u043c \u201e\u0421\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0430 \u0421\u043e\u0444\u0438\u044f\u201c). It was constructed in the 6th century on top of existing places of worship, which dated all the way back to the Roman city of Serdika, during the second century. It is believed that during the 14th century, it gave its name to the capital. Finally, the church peeking through the trees is St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2233\/2084851351.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The statue of the best known Bulgarian writer Ivan Vazov, often referred to as \"the Patriarch of Bulgarian literature\". The reddish building in the background is Temple Sveta Sofia (\u0425\u0440\u0430\u043c \u201e\u0421\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0430 \u0421\u043e\u0444\u0438\u044f\u201c). It was constructed in the 6th century on top of existing places of worship, which dated all the way back to the Roman city of Serdika, during the second century. It is believed that during the 14th century, it gave its name to the capital. Finally, the church peeking through the trees is St. Alexander Nevsky Cathedral.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2236.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"National Art Gallery (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0445\u0443\u0434\u043e\u0436\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u0430 \u0433\u0430\u043b\u0435\u0440\u0438\u044f, &lt;i&gt;Natsionalna hudozhestvena galeriya&lt;\/i&gt;) was formerly the royal palace. After the fall of the Monarchy as part of the settlement at the end of the Second World War, the art gallery moved into this building as its initial building had gotten bombed during the war.\" data-attachment-id=\"1524\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"National Art Gallery (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0445\u0443\u0434\u043e\u0436\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u0430 \u0433\u0430\u043b\u0435\u0440\u0438\u044f, &lt;i&gt;Natsionalna hudozhestvena galeriya&lt;\/i&gt;) was formerly the royal palace. After the fall of the Monarchy as part of the settlement at the end of the Second World War, the art gallery moved into this building as its initial building had gotten bombed during the war.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2236\/4215692169.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">National Art Gallery (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0445\u0443\u0434\u043e\u0436\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u0430 \u0433\u0430\u043b\u0435\u0440\u0438\u044f, <i>Natsionalna hudozhestvena galeriya<\/i>) was formerly the royal palace. After the fall of the Monarchy as part of the settlement at the end of the Second World War, the art gallery moved into this building as its initial building had gotten bombed during the war.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2240.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Ivan Vazov National Theatre (\u041d\u0430\u0440\u043e\u0434\u0435\u043d \u0442\u0435\u0430\u0442\u044a\u0440 \u201e\u0418\u0432\u0430\u043d \u0412\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u201c, &lt;i&gt;Naroden teatar \u201eIvan Vazov\u201c&lt;\/i&gt;) is the oldest theater in Bulgaria, dating back to 1904. Unfortunately in 1923, a major fire destroyed the interior of the theater. The government invested in getting a rotational stage that includes a swimming pool underneath. It was reopened in 1927 and all of that technology is still being used. The reconstruction of the stage was made by Germans so would you expect any less? (This is an example of a common joke showing Bulgaria\u2019s high regard for German engineering.)\n&lt;p&gt;During the restoration of the fa\u00e7ade of the theater, the gilder took a couple creative freedoms. You see, the artist\u2019s wife was pregnant and the artist wanted a boy. He was told that if he painted the penises of all the boy angles in gold, he would have a boy. Do you think he took that advice?\" data-attachment-id=\"1526\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Ivan Vazov National Theatre (\u041d\u0430\u0440\u043e\u0434\u0435\u043d \u0442\u0435\u0430\u0442\u044a\u0440 \u201e\u0418\u0432\u0430\u043d \u0412\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u201c, &lt;i&gt;Naroden teatar \u201eIvan Vazov\u201c&lt;\/i&gt;) is the oldest theater in Bulgaria, dating back to 1904. Unfortunately in 1923, a major fire destroyed the interior of the theater. The government invested in getting a rotational stage that includes a swimming pool underneath. It was reopened in 1927 and all of that technology is still being used. The reconstruction of the stage was made by Germans so would you expect any less? (This is an example of a common joke showing Bulgaria\u2019s high regard for German engineering.)\n&lt;p&gt;During the restoration of the fa\u00e7ade of the theater, the gilder took a couple creative freedoms. You see, the artist\u2019s wife was pregnant and the artist wanted a boy. He was told that if he painted the penises of all the boy angles in gold, he would have a boy. Do you think he took that advice?\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2240\/3381652758.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Ivan Vazov National Theatre (\u041d\u0430\u0440\u043e\u0434\u0435\u043d \u0442\u0435\u0430\u0442\u044a\u0440 \u201e\u0418\u0432\u0430\u043d \u0412\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u201c, <i>Naroden teatar \u201eIvan Vazov\u201c<\/i>) is the oldest theater in Bulgaria, dating back to 1904. Unfortunately in 1923, a major fire destroyed the interior of the theater. The government invested in getting a rotational stage that includes a swimming pool underneath. It was reopened in 1927 and all of that technology is still being used. The reconstruction of the stage was made by Germans so would you expect any less? (This is an example of a common joke showing Bulgaria\u2019s high regard for German engineering.)\n<p>During the restoration of the fa\u00e7ade of the theater, the gilder took a couple creative freedoms. You see, the artist\u2019s wife was pregnant and the artist wanted a boy. He was told that if he painted the penises of all the boy angles in gold, he would have a boy. Do you think he took that advice?<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2250.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Currently, this building houses the National Archaeological Museum (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u0435\u043d \u0410\u0440\u0445\u0435\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438 \u043c\u0443\u0437\u0435\u0439, &lt;i&gt;Natsionalen arheologicheski muzey&lt;\/i&gt;). The building, however, was first built as a mosque in 1494. It then served as a library and only in 1893 (five years after the liberation) did it start to be converted into the National Archaeological Museum. It was officially opened as such in 1905.\n&lt;p&gt;If you look closely, you will notice that the ground floor of the building has actually completely sunk underground. This is because the mosque was built on swampy lands. A door was created on the second floor and it now serves as a main floor.\" data-attachment-id=\"1528\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Currently, this building houses the National Archaeological Museum (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u0435\u043d \u0410\u0440\u0445\u0435\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438 \u043c\u0443\u0437\u0435\u0439, &lt;i&gt;Natsionalen arheologicheski muzey&lt;\/i&gt;). The building, however, was first built as a mosque in 1494. It then served as a library and only in 1893 (five years after the liberation) did it start to be converted into the National Archaeological Museum. It was officially opened as such in 1905.\n&lt;p&gt;If you look closely, you will notice that the ground floor of the building has actually completely sunk underground. This is because the mosque was built on swampy lands. A door was created on the second floor and it now serves as a main floor.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2250\/2588002784.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Currently, this building houses the National Archaeological Museum (\u041d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u0435\u043d \u0410\u0440\u0445\u0435\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438 \u043c\u0443\u0437\u0435\u0439, <i>Natsionalen arheologicheski muzey<\/i>). The building, however, was first built as a mosque in 1494. It then served as a library and only in 1893 (five years after the liberation) did it start to be converted into the National Archaeological Museum. It was officially opened as such in 1905.\n<p>If you look closely, you will notice that the ground floor of the building has actually completely sunk underground. This is because the mosque was built on swampy lands. A door was created on the second floor and it now serves as a main floor.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2248.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Guard change in front of the Presidential Offices.\" data-attachment-id=\"1527\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Guard change in front of the Presidential Offices.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2248\/3216088299.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Guard change in front of the Presidential Offices.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2278.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The triangular structure in the middle of the picture is the Former Communist Party House. It now serves as administrative offices for the National Assembly. The Former Communist Party House is flanked on both sides by identical and rather controversial buildings. The building to the left houses the National Council on Ethnic and Demographic Issues, the Ministry of Labour and Social Politics and the Agency for Social Assistance. In contrast, the building on the right houses a gentleman&#039;s bar, a casino, a luxury hotel and the offices of the President of the Republic of Bulgaria. As if that wasn&#039;t odd enough, in the courtyard of this second building, you will find the Church St. George Rotunda. Why did the government and businesses chose to have such different organizations right next to each other? Most likely because those were the buildings available at the time and most appropriately located.\" data-attachment-id=\"1536\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The triangular structure in the middle of the picture is the Former Communist Party House. It now serves as administrative offices for the National Assembly. The Former Communist Party House is flanked on both sides by identical and rather controversial buildings. The building to the left houses the National Council on Ethnic and Demographic Issues, the Ministry of Labour and Social Politics and the Agency for Social Assistance. In contrast, the building on the right houses a gentleman&#039;s bar, a casino, a luxury hotel and the offices of the President of the Republic of Bulgaria. As if that wasn&#039;t odd enough, in the courtyard of this second building, you will find the Church St. George Rotunda. Why did the government and businesses chose to have such different organizations right next to each other? Most likely because those were the buildings available at the time and most appropriately located.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2278\/1531304110.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The triangular structure in the middle of the picture is the Former Communist Party House. It now serves as administrative offices for the National Assembly. The Former Communist Party House is flanked on both sides by identical and rather controversial buildings. The building to the left houses the National Council on Ethnic and Demographic Issues, the Ministry of Labour and Social Politics and the Agency for Social Assistance. In contrast, the building on the right houses a gentleman's bar, a casino, a luxury hotel and the offices of the President of the Republic of Bulgaria. As if that wasn't odd enough, in the courtyard of this second building, you will find the Church St. George Rotunda. Why did the government and businesses chose to have such different organizations right next to each other? Most likely because those were the buildings available at the time and most appropriately located.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2261.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Central Mineral Baths (\u0426\u0435\u043d\u0442\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u043c\u0438\u043d\u0435\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0431\u0430\u043d\u044f, &lt;i&gt; Tsentralna mineralna banya&lt;\/i&gt;) was built in 1908. It served as the city\u2019s main public bath using natural thermal water springs until 1986 when it was closed for restoration. In September 2015, it was reopened as the Museum of Sofia.\" data-attachment-id=\"1530\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Central Mineral Baths (\u0426\u0435\u043d\u0442\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u043c\u0438\u043d\u0435\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0431\u0430\u043d\u044f, &lt;i&gt; Tsentralna mineralna banya&lt;\/i&gt;) was built in 1908. It served as the city\u2019s main public bath using natural thermal water springs until 1986 when it was closed for restoration. In September 2015, it was reopened as the Museum of Sofia.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2261\/961062644.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Central Mineral Baths (\u0426\u0435\u043d\u0442\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u043c\u0438\u043d\u0435\u0440\u0430\u043b\u043d\u0430 \u0431\u0430\u043d\u044f, <i> Tsentralna mineralna banya<\/i>) was built in 1908. It served as the city\u2019s main public bath using natural thermal water springs until 1986 when it was closed for restoration. In September 2015, it was reopened as the Museum of Sofia.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/Square-of-Tolerance.png\" data-caption-title=\"Right in the center of the Bulgarian capital, we call this the square of tolerance as four of the major religions are represented. Some even joke to say that five major religions are represented, if you include McDonalds representing the capitalism religion.\" data-attachment-id=\"1549\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Right in the center of the Bulgarian capital, we call this the square of tolerance as four of the major religions are represented. Some even joke to say that five major religions are represented, if you include McDonalds representing the capitalism religion.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/Square-of-Tolerance\/2833279686.png\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Right in the center of the Bulgarian capital, we call this the square of tolerance as four of the major religions are represented. Some even joke to say that five major religions are represented, if you include McDonalds representing the capitalism religion.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2272.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Sofia Synagogue was opened in 1909 in the presence of Tsar Ferdinand I. It is the largest synagogue in southeast Europe and the third largest in all of Europe. Despite the massive size, only about fifty people use it regularly.\n&lt;p&gt;The Synagogue also houses the Jewish Museum. During the Second World War, Bulgaria was on the side of the Germans due to familial ties of the Royal Family. Regardless, when Hitler demanded that all of the Bulgarian Jews be deported, the Bulgarian people did not like the news. Seeing the unrest among his people, the Bulgarian Tsar lied and said that he had created a concentration camp to help Bulgaria with the war efforts. It was a ploy to earn more time. The day after Tsar Boris came back from a meeting with Hitler, he died. It is rumored that Hitler poisoned him. Nonetheless, the war ended before Hitler could check whether or not the concentration camp actually existed and Tsar Boris is estimated to have saved 50,000 lives.\" data-attachment-id=\"1533\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Sofia Synagogue was opened in 1909 in the presence of Tsar Ferdinand I. It is the largest synagogue in southeast Europe and the third largest in all of Europe. Despite the massive size, only about fifty people use it regularly.\n&lt;p&gt;The Synagogue also houses the Jewish Museum. During the Second World War, Bulgaria was on the side of the Germans due to familial ties of the Royal Family. Regardless, when Hitler demanded that all of the Bulgarian Jews be deported, the Bulgarian people did not like the news. Seeing the unrest among his people, the Bulgarian Tsar lied and said that he had created a concentration camp to help Bulgaria with the war efforts. It was a ploy to earn more time. The day after Tsar Boris came back from a meeting with Hitler, he died. It is rumored that Hitler poisoned him. Nonetheless, the war ended before Hitler could check whether or not the concentration camp actually existed and Tsar Boris is estimated to have saved 50,000 lives.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2017\/04\/DSC_2272\/2510294477.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Sofia Synagogue was opened in 1909 in the presence of Tsar Ferdinand I. It is the largest synagogue in southeast Europe and the third largest in all of Europe. Despite the massive size, only about fifty people use it regularly.\n<p>The Synagogue also houses the Jewish Museum. During the Second World War, Bulgaria was on the side of the Germans due to familial ties of the Royal Family. Regardless, when Hitler demanded that all of the Bulgarian Jews be deported, the Bulgarian people did not like the news. Seeing the unrest among his people, the Bulgarian Tsar lied and said that he had created a concentration camp to help Bulgaria with the war efforts. It was a ploy to earn more time. The day after Tsar Boris came back from a meeting with Hitler, he died. It is rumored that Hitler poisoned him. Nonetheless, the war ended before Hitler could check whether or not the concentration camp actually existed and Tsar Boris is estimated to have saved 50,000 lives.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/04\/DSC_2268.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In the foreground of the picture to the right is the Banya Bashi Mosque (\u0411\u0430\u043d\u044f \u0431\u0430\u0448\u0438 \u0434\u0436\u0430\u043c\u0438\u044f, &lt;i&gt;Banya bashi dzhamiya&lt;\/i&gt;), built in 1566. To the left are the Roman excavations found during the construction of the second metro line. Finally, in the background on the left is the 